<title>UK: UK Q1 1997 GDP rises final 0.9 percent q/q.</title>
<headline>UK Q1 1997 GDP rises final 0.9 percent q/q.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-06-27</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Office for National Statistics released the following gross domestic product data for Q1 1997. GDP (FACTOR COST, 1990 PRICES)</p>
<p>			    Q1,97	   Q4,96	   Q1,96</p>
<p>    Index		 111.8 (110.8) 110.9 (109.8) 108.4(107.6)</p>
<p>    pct change q/q	0.9 (0.9)     1.1 (0.8)     0.6 (0.6)</p>
<p>    pct change y/y	3.1 (3.0)     2.9 (2.6)     2.1 (2.1)</p>
<p>GDP AT MARKET PRICES (MONEY GDP)</p>
<p>    Index		 139.5	   137.9	   132.3</p>
<p>    Pct change on qtr   1.2	     2.1	     1.8</p>
<p>    Pct change yr/yr    5.5	     6.1	     5.4</p>
<p>GDP EX OIL AND GAS EXTRACTION (CONSTANT PRICES)</p>
<p>    Pct change on qtr   1.0	     1.1	     0.6</p>
<p>    Pct change yr/yr    3.1	     2.8	     2.1</p>
<p>PERSONAL SECTOR INCOME, SPENDING, SAVING</p>
<p>					(million stg)    QQ%    YY%</p>
<p> Total personal pre-tax income    172,283	0.7   3.3</p>
<p> Wages,salaries,forces pay	   90,886	1.9   6.1</p>
<p> Total personal disposable income 136,987	0.2   3.0</p>
<p>Consumer expenditure (current</p>
<p> market prices)			 122,691	1.3   5.9</p>
<p>  Real personal disposable income 107,664     -0.2   0.9</p>
<p>	 (% change QQ)		  Q1	  Q4	 Q1/Q1 96</p>
<p>  Expenditure deflator		0.4	 N/A	2.1</p>
<p>Implied GDP deflator (at</p>
<p>  factor cost)			  0.1	 1.0	2.2</p>
<p>Consumer spending (constant</p>
<p>  1990 prices)			  0.9	 N/A	3.7</p>
<p>					    Q1	  Q4	 Q1,96</p>
<p>  Personal saving (million stg)	 14,296    15,605   17,138</p>
<p>  Savings ratio (pct)		 10.4	 11.4     12.9</p>
<p>A Reuter poll of economists found a consensus expecting Q1 GDP growth to be unrevised at 0.9 percent on the quarter and 3.0 percent year-on-year.</p>
<p>The ONS said the path of GDP in 1996 had been revised to show a higher level of growth in the last quarter of 1996.</p>
<p>GDP growth in Q4 was revised to 1.1 percent from 0.8 percent quarter-on-quarter and to 2.9 percent from 2.6 percent year-on-year.</p>
<p>Revised estimates showed GDP growing at 4.5 percent in 1994, revised from 4.0 percent previously, and 2.8 percent in 1995, revised from 2.5 percent.</p>
</text>
